Z||000000||HummelHund||09-30-2001||12:06 AM||||84 SE 4cyl, 4 speed. Two predominant noises: A roar (wheel bearing?), and a "clanking chain" noise. On the first, any heads up on changing the rear whl bearings? On the second, it literally sounds like metal rings being struck together. Can hear it real good when you've accelerated and let off gas. On first noise - goes away on right hand turns. Help!!!!!||||reg||11
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-30-2001||01:13 AM||||Hey Hund!<BR>Definately sounds like rear wheel bearings...<BR>I have done mine, and it is fairly straight forward...<BR>Once you decide to do it, you need to remove the large nut off the axle shaft, then the wheel, and then everything down to the hub assembly...<BR>Three bolts hold the hub assy in place, and the hub slides off the axle...<BR>Fairly easy, just takes time and the proper tools...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||HummelHund||10-01-2001||09:37 AM||||====bump=== little help guys!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||10-01-2001||09:46 AM||||Second sound!<BR>Check to see if the harmonic balancer outer ring has seperated. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||HummelHund||10-01-2001||08:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wolfhound:<BR><B>Second sound!<BR>Check to see if the harmonic balancer outer ring has seperated. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Whoa - that sounds very deep. I've never had any experience with that. I'll have to get the manual out for that one. Hmmm... may be time to get another Merkur XR4Ti||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||10-01-2001||09:59 PM||||What's so deep about that! The ringing on acceleration and when decelerating or the engine is cut off was the symptoms I had. it,s not noticeable visually because its captured behind the pulley. looks normal when the engine is stopped. I like to have never found it.<P>Your reply sounds like you think I'm pulling your leg. Not the case. here is a photo of the separated balance if you can't find your manual.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/whirl_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HummelHund||10-02-2001||12:12 AM||||LOL. Nope, it's just I hadn't heard of one on a Fiero. It sounded like my "flux capacitor" was bad. I only have the Haynes and Chilton manuals, and Haynes refers to it twice as a "vibration damper", then in classic Haynes fashion, doesn't show it! I really appreciate the picture. Makes sense, since the sound is like striking two metal rings together.<BR>Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000007||HummelHund||10-02-2001||12:14 AM||||BTW - what was involved in pulling it, and where and how much does a replacement come from? The Fiero Store catalog didn't list one at all. TIA||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||10-02-2001||05:55 AM||||HummelHund. The part that can loosen is the outer ring with your timing mark on it. Check it first.  If this is the problem it will spin by hand on the crank. If you have impact tools and a puller it's a breeze.<P>It could be number of other things. Heat shield loose, Something loose on the exhaust system, AC clutch, etc.<BR>Coy<p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroSoul||10-02-2001||06:26 AM|||| Before you chase too far down the Harmonic balancer road...is this an original 84 2.5?  If so, according to what I have read on one site, it doesn't have a balancer on it. <BR>  Could the second noise be a bad CV joint instead?  Possible the same axel as the bad wheel bearing?||||reg||3
Z||000010||Wolfhound||10-02-2001||06:39 AM||||FieroSoul, It,s not a chase down a road. Just reach down and see if you can spin it on the crank by hand. It,s the least likely problem. I mentioned it because it,s hard to find. no 84,s were internally balanced.<P>You're right it could be the CV joint.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
